Server is inside the JVM:

    java -jar app.jar
    └── SpringApplication.run()
        └── context refresh
            └── instantiate Tomcat (org.apache.catalina.startup.Tomcat)
                └── bind port, deploy DispatcherServlet, start accept

# Graceful shutdown -- application.properties
server.shutdown=graceful
spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase=30s

# Kubernetes -- must exceed shutdown timeout
#   terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45

# Sizing -- must be coordinated
server.tomcat.max-threads=200
spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=20
# 200 threads waiting on 20 connections = 180 blocked per burst

// Clean shutdown for background processors
@Component
class KafkaListenerLifecycle implements SmartLifecycle {
    private final MessageListenerContainer container;
    private volatile boolean running;

    KafkaListenerLifecycle(MessageListenerContainer container) {
        this.container = container;
    }

    public void start() { container.start(); running = true; }

    public void stop() {
        container.stop();       // stops polling, commits offsets cleanly
        running = false;
    }

    public boolean isRunning() { return running; }
}

// Clean shutdown for executors
@PreDestroy
void shutdown() throws InterruptedException {
    executor.shutdown();
    if (!executor.awaitTermination(20, TimeUnit.SECONDS))
        executor.shutdownNow();
}

At-most-once (data loss possible):
  consumer: enable.auto.commit=true  (commit before process)
  producer: acks=0

At-least-once (duplicates possible, no loss):
  consumer: enable.auto.commit=false, process then commitSync()
  producer: acks=all, retries>0
  requirement: every consumer handler must be idempotent

Exactly-once (Kafka-internal):
  producer: enable.idempotence=true, transactional.id=<unique>
  consumer: isolation.level=read_committed
  pattern: consume-transform-produce inside a transaction,
           commit the consumed offset in the same transaction

  try (Producer p = ...) {
      p.initTransactions();
      try {
          p.beginTransaction();
          for (Record r : consumer.poll(...)) {
              p.send(transform(r));
              offsets.put(partition, r.offset()+1);
          }
          p.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, groupId);
          p.commitTransaction();
      } catch (Exception e) {
          p.abortTransaction();
      }
  }

Boundary: Kafka EOS does not cover writes to external DBs/APIs.
          Use idempotent writes or transactional outbox for those.

Failure windows

  1. poll() returns records 100..199
  2. process 100..149  (side effects done)
  3. CRASH before commit
  4. restart -> committed offset still 100
  5. records 100..149 reprocessed -> duplicate side effects

Auto-commit variant (data loss):
  1. poll() returns 100..199
  2. auto-commit fires -> commits 200
  3. CRASH while processing 150
  4. restart -> fetch from 200 -> 150..199 LOST

Safe consumer loop

  props.put("enable.auto.commit", "false");

  while (running) {
      records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
      for (record : records) {
          processIdempotently(record);   // dedup by eventId / business key
      }
      consumer.commitSync();            // commit AFTER processing
  }

Inbox pattern (offset + side effect atomically)

  BEGIN;
    INSERT INTO inbox(event_id) VALUES (:id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
    -- if inserted, apply domain change
    UPDATE orders SET status='PAID' WHERE id=:orderId;
  COMMIT;
  consumer.commitSync();   // commit Kafka offset after DB commit

Producer idempotence (session-scoped, broker-side):

  enable.idempotence=true
  -> PID assigned to producer session
  -> per-partition sequence number on each batch
  -> broker drops duplicate (PID, partition, sequence)
  -> does NOT survive producer restart (new PID)

Consumer idempotency (application-level):

  // idempotency key from the record
  String key = record.headers().lastHeader("eventId").value();
  // or business key: orderId + operation

  // inbox table with unique constraint
  BEGIN;
    INSERT INTO inbox(event_id) VALUES (:key)
      ON CONFLICT (event_id) DO NOTHING;   -- duplicate -> 0 rows inserted

    if (rowsInserted == 1) {
        applyDomainChange(record);           // only on first processing
    }
  COMMIT;
  consumer.commitSync();

Bad idempotency key:

  topic + partition + offset
  -> offset changes across replays and cluster moves
  -> same logical message gets a new key -> duplicate side effect

External API idempotency:

  POST /charges
  Idempotency-Key: <eventId UUID>
  -> retry with same key returns original response, no double charge

// Producer
props.put("transactional.id", "payment-service-0");  // stable across restarts
producer.initTransactions();

try {
    producer.beginTransaction();
    for (record : consumer.poll(...)) {
        producer.send(transform(record));
        offsets.put(partition, record.offset() + 1);
    }
    producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, groupId);  // atomic offset commit
    producer.commitTransaction();
} catch (Exception e) {
    producer.abortTransaction();
}

// Consumer
props.put("isolation.level", "read_committed");  // hides aborted records

// Zombie fencing
// Producer A (epoch 5) pauses -> Producer B (epoch 6) starts with same transactional.id
// A wakes up, tries commitTransaction -> ProducerFencedException
// only the latest epoch can commit

Boundary:
  Kafka topic -> Kafka topic: EOS covers it
  Kafka topic -> PostgreSQL: outside the transaction; need idempotent writes or outbox

